      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the morphological difference of coronary atherosclerotic plaques in patients with stable angina(SA), unstable angina (UA) and acute myocardial infarction (AMI)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "101 autopsy cases of patients with SA, UA and/or AMI were studied using routine histological and immunohistochemical staining."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Coronary atherosclerotic plaques in SA patients were mainly the fibrous plaques with no or just very small necrotic cores; rich in smooth muscle cells and collagen fibers, less foamy cells (stable plaque), low incidence of plaque rupture (14% only) and no thrombosis found. The atherosclerotic plaques in UA and AMI patients were mainly the atheroma (unstable plaque) with large necrotic core (> 40%), thin fibrous cap, less smooth muscle cells and abundant foamy cells. The incidences of plaque rupture were 76% and 82%, thrombosis 81% and 91% respectively in 58 cases of UA and 22 cases of AMI, and the incidence of UA is statistically significant in comparing with that of the SA group (P < 0.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In SA group, stable plaque was the main finding, plaque rupture and thrombosis rare. While in UA and AMI patients, unstable plaques predominant with a high incidence of plaque rupture and thrombosis which were the leading cause of acute coronary events."
        }
      ]},
